# 1. Distributed Web Infrastructure

## 1. User Request Journey & Diagram

When a user wants to access `www.foobar.com`:
1. The user types `www.foobar.com` into their web browser.
2. The browser requests a DNS server to resolve `www.foobar.com` into the IP address of the Load Balancer (`8.8.8.8`).
3. The DNS server responds with IP `8.8.8.8`.
4. The client browser sends an HTTP request to the Load Balancer (HAproxy) at IP `8.8.8.8`.
5. HAproxy receives the request and forwards it to one of the two web servers (Server 2 or Server 3) using a load-balancing algorithm.
6. The selected Web Server (Nginx) processes static content or routes dynamic requests to the local Application Server.
7. The Application Server processes application logic, executing **Write** operations on the **MySQL Primary** node and **Read** operations on the **MySQL Replica** node.
8. The response flows back through the Web Server and HAproxy to the user's browser.

