2. Specifics of the Infrastructure
For every additional element, why you are adding it:
Load Balancer (HAproxy): Added to manage and distribute incoming user traffic across multiple web servers, preventing a single server from being overwhelmed and eliminating traffic bottlenecks.

Second Application/Web Server: Added to achieve horizontal scaling (handling more concurrent requests) and to provide redundancy if one server crashes.

Database Replica (MySQL Slave): Added to offload Read traffic from the Primary database, improving application performance and creating a real-time data backup.

What distribution algorithm your load balancer is configured with and how it works:
Round Robin: HAproxy is configured with the Round Robin algorithm. It works by distributing incoming client requests sequentially across the list of available backend web servers in a repeating cycle (e.g., Request 1 -> Server 2, Request 2 -> Server 3, Request 3 -> Server 2, and so on).

Is your load-balancer enabling an Active-Active or Active-Passive setup?
The Load Balancer enables an Active-Active setup for the web servers.

Difference between Active-Active and Active-Passive:

Active-Active: All configured backend servers actively process incoming user traffic simultaneously, sharing the overall load.

Active-Passive: Only one server (Active) processes incoming traffic while the backup server (Passive/Standby) remains idle, taking over only when the active server fails.

How a database Primary-Replica (Master-Slave) cluster works:
In a Primary-Replica cluster, data synchronization occurs asynchronously:

All data modifications (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) are written to the Primary database node.

The Primary records these changes in a Binary Log (Binlog).

The Replica node continuously reads the Primary's binary log and executes those same changes on its local database copy to remain synchronized.

What is the difference between the Primary node and the Replica node in regard to the application:
Primary Node (Master): Handles all Read and Write operations from the application. It is the single source of truth for dynamic data modification.

Replica Node (Slave): Handles Read-Only operations from the application (such as fetching user profiles, reading posts). The application cannot execute write queries directly to the Replica node.

3. Issues with this Infrastructure
Single Points of Failure (SPOF):

HAproxy (Load Balancer): There is only one Load Balancer server. If Server 1 crashes or goes offline, the entire website becomes completely inaccessible.

MySQL Primary Database: If Server 2 fails, the MySQL Primary node goes down, rendering the application unable to execute any Write operations.

Security Issues:

No Firewall: Servers are not protected by a firewall (such as UFW or iptables), leaving ports exposed to unauthorized access and security threats.

No HTTPS (Unencrypted Traffic): Traffic between client browsers and HAproxy uses plain HTTP (Port 80) without SSL/TLS certificates, exposing data to packet sniffing and Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks.

No Monitoring:

There are no monitoring systems (such as Datadog, Nagios, or Prometheus) installed to track system health, CPU/RAM usage, network traffic, or service uptime. As a result, administrators will not receive automated alerts if services crash or experience performance degradation.